#---
# purpose: "`#{...}` in a string literal evaluates and is rendered, instead of being copied through verbatim"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# # string_interpolation
#
#     dbg("a#{1}b")
#
#     fz:      "a#{1}b"
#     Elixir:  "a1b"
#
# The lexer copied the bytes of a literal through verbatim and had no `#{`
# handling anywhere, so interpolation silently did nothing. That is the worst
# shape a bug can take -- a plausible-looking answer -- and it is why a String
# module could not ship before this: every error message the library built
# would have been wrong in the same silent way.
#
# THE DESUGARING IS ELIXIR'S OWN, and it happens in the LEXER:
#
#     "a#{x}b"   ->   "a" <> Kernel.to_string(x) <> "b"
#
# Doing it at the token level means nothing downstream learns a new node: by
# the time the parser sees it, an interpolated string is ordinary concat and an
# ordinary call. It is also why interpolation composes with everything without
# further work -- any expression, because the segment is lexed as source.
#
# Empty literal pieces are dropped, so `"#{x}"` is one call and not a concat
# with two empty binaries either side.
#
# `\#{` is an escape, so a literal `#{` can still be written, and `inspect`
# escapes it back on the way out so the rendering round-trips as source.
def main() do
  x = 1
  name = "ada"

  dbg("a#{x}b")
  dbg("#{x}")
  dbg("no interp")
  dbg("sum #{1 + 2} done")
  dbg("atom #{:hi}")
  dbg("float #{2.5}")
  dbg("bool #{true}")
  dbg("nil #{nil}")
  dbg("binary #{name}")
  dbg("two #{x} and #{name}")
  dbg("nested call #{Kernel.to_string(x)} ok")
  dbg("list #{Kernel.to_string(x)}#{name}")
  dbg("escaped \#{x}")
end
